WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:06.730
Hey, David, can someone who didn't win the
Iowa caucus still win the nomination?

00:00:06.730 --> 00:00:07.730
Absolutely.

00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:15.139
I've said from the start, especially because
of the compression of you've got Iowa was

00:00:15.139 --> 00:00:18.869
Monday, February 3rd, we've then gotten New
Hampshire.

00:00:18.869 --> 00:00:23.619
We then have a Nevada, then we South Carolina,
and then all within a month.

00:00:23.619 --> 00:00:27.140
Then it's super Tuesday, March 3rd, where
you have a whole bunch of delegates.

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:30.860
The importance of the first couple is mostly
PR.

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:37.500
The PR was a disaster because of the completely
failed way in which the DNC carried out the

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:40.090
caucus, the failure of the app that they developed.

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:44.629
I mean it just an embarrassing, complete and
total mess, which allowed multiple candidates

00:00:44.629 --> 00:00:46.600
to essentially claim that they won it.

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:49.379
Let Donald Trump laugh at everybody on Twitter,
which he did very, very successfully.

00:00:49.379 --> 00:00:52.359
Quite frankly, it really played into his hands.

00:00:52.359 --> 00:01:00.469
But the 41 delegates from Iowa are not make
or break in any way other than, uh, justifying

00:01:00.469 --> 00:01:04.150
from a PR standpoint that you are viable,
right?

00:01:04.150 --> 00:01:10.189
Getting some delegates in Iowa gets you past
zero and that's what matters.

00:01:10.189 --> 00:01:12.890
But you know, Iowa is only 41 delegates.

00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:20.640
Um, New Hampshire is 24 delegates, even smaller
than Nevada is 36 delegates, also smaller

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:22.680
than Iowa, but not by much.

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:29.579
The largest of the first four primaries is
South Carolina where 54 delegates are awarded.

00:01:29.579 --> 00:01:35.689
But remember with proportional allocation,
if you get 25% of the vote and someone else

00:01:35.689 --> 00:01:40.680
gets 20% of the vote, you both end up with
almost the same number of delegates.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:44.729
Now the thing I would be more concerned about
at this point, the primaries are going to

00:01:44.729 --> 00:01:50.210
happen and the delegate counts are going to
start to add up and only after super Tuesday

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:54.439
are the pads going to start to mathematically
get harder and harder.

00:01:54.439 --> 00:01:59.470
After super Tuesday we will be able to make
calculations like for example, based on all

00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:05.979
of the voting so far in order for the third
place person to win, they would have to get,

00:02:05.979 --> 00:02:10.940
you know, call it 50% of all remaining delegates
are making the numbers up and then we would

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:15.920
be able to start to say, well, they've only
gotten 12% of the delegates so far.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:20.460
How likely is it that from here on out they
get half of the remaining delegates and even

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:26.750
though they will stay and we will be able
to start mathematically closing the road so

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:30.210
to speak, the path will begin to close for
a lot of different candidates.

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:31.210
For now.

00:02:31.210 --> 00:02:32.210
It's all wide open.

00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:33.690
Here's the really important thing.

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:37.030
What I want to see is what the DNC pulse,
okay.

00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:38.770
We've been talking about this for a while.

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:42.770
When it comes to superdelegates, when it comes
to the debate requirements at the beginning

00:02:42.770 --> 00:02:46.060
of the week, I told you the DNC is doing the
thing we thought they might do for a long

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:52.000
time, which is they're changing the debate
qualification requirements in a way that would

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:53.490
make one really important change.

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:58.510
It would allow Michael Bloomberg to participate
in the debates even without accepting individual

00:02:58.510 --> 00:02:59.510
donors.

00:02:59.510 --> 00:03:01.180
By removing that as a requirement.

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:06.670
There have started to be all sorts of rumblings
about, you know, is John Kerry going to swoop

00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:09.150
in as Joe Biden fails?

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:13.130
I don't think that's going to happen, but
it's a conversation that was floating around.

00:03:13.130 --> 00:03:16.980
Will the DNC put all of its weight behind
a different establishment candidate?

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:22.380
We saw that the establishment candidates by,
by voting, um, uh, entrance polls in the Iowa

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:29.400
caucuses, um, are Biden boot edge edge and
Klobuchar will the DNC switch.

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:34.160
But most importantly, there are now a number
of reports including one we did on the Tuesday

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:40.020
bonus show saying that the NC executives are
talking about can we put super delegates back

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:41.420
in the first round of voting.

00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:44.330
And to understand the importance of that,
you have to understand what happened back

00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:50.630
in 2016 back in 2016 superdelegates, which
are party insiders and other sort of higher

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:54.070
ups designated people in the D in the democratic
party.

00:03:54.070 --> 00:04:00.560
They got to vote in addition to the delegates
that were won in primaries at the democratic

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:01.590
national convention.

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:06.560
So as Bernie and Hillary were fighting it
out and they were close and they were one

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:11.520
of the, we knew all along that because all
of these super delegates were pledged to Hillary

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:15.200
Clinton, that once you get to that first vote
at the convention, no matter what happens

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:19.790
in the primaries, even if it's tight, Hillary
gets the nomination because of the super delegates,

00:04:19.790 --> 00:04:21.800
incredibly unfair and undemocratic.

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:26.440
So since 2016 the democratic party agreed
not to allow super delegates to vote in the

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:28.210
first round at the DNC.

00:04:28.210 --> 00:04:34.180
So the idea is the delegates vote in the first
round at the democratic convention based on

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:35.940
state results.

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:41.770
If no one wins on the first round, then they
do a second round where super delegates get

00:04:41.770 --> 00:04:42.770
to vote.

00:04:42.770 --> 00:04:50.170
That was what we knew up until Monday, starting
Tuesday after the Iowa fiasco, DNC executives

00:04:50.170 --> 00:04:54.830
have started to have a conversation about
could we maybe go back on that?

00:04:54.830 --> 00:04:58.370
Could we maybe get the super delegates back
into voting in the first round?

00:04:58.370 --> 00:05:02.690
They seem to recognize that it would enrage
millions of people and rightly so because

00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:05.330
it's completely anti-democratic.

00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:09.840
But there is a conversation that is taking
place and I would be looking at that more

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:14.030
than who has the mathematical path right now
because Iowa, New Hampshire, it's going to

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:16.180
be wide open going into super Tuesday.

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:18.570
Um, uh, that's for sure.

00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:19.130
Let's see what the DNC plans to do.

